WEBVTT - Most Fascinating Stories - Episode 212: The United Kingdom: Stonehenge, Roman Britannia, and the Wrath of Queen Boudica

1
00:00:03.040 --> 00:00:05.680
[Rebecca] Welcome to Most Fascinating Stories.

2
00:00:05.680 --> 00:00:06.880
[Rebecca] I'm Rebecca.

3
00:00:06.880 --> 00:00:13.120
[Rebecca] Today we begin an epic new journey across the European continent, the major European nation series.

4
00:00:13.120 --> 00:00:18.960
[Rebecca] We start on the misty, storm-swept island that built a global empire, the United Kingdom.

5
00:00:18.960 --> 00:00:28.640
[Rebecca] Here is George with the mystery of Stonehenge, the Roman invasion, and the legendary revolt of Queen Boudica.

6
00:00:28.640 --> 00:00:40.240
[George] Five thousand years ago, long before the first stone was cut for the Great Pyramid of Giza, ancient Britons began constructing one of the most enigmatic monuments on Earth, Stonehenge.

7
00:00:40.240 --> 00:00:50.080
[George] Rising from the windswept chalk plateau of Salisbury Plain in Wiltshire, Stonehenge was constructed in three distinct phases spanning 1,500 years.

8
00:00:50.080 --> 00:01:00.960
[George] The builders began around 3000 BC by digging a circular earthen ditch and bank with deer antlers, accompanied by 56 mysterious pits known as Aubrey Holes.

9
00:01:00.960 --> 00:01:06.320
[George] Around 2500 BC, an extraordinary logistical miracle occurred.

10
00:01:06.320 --> 00:01:20.480
[George] Prehistoric engineers transported 80 massive bluestones, each weighing up to four tons, over 150 miles from the Priscelli Hills in southwestern Wales, moving them across rough waterways and rolling timber tracks.

11
00:01:20.480 --> 00:01:32.800
[George] Later, colossal Sarson sandstone megaliths, weighing between 25 and 50 tons, were hauled from the Marlborough Downs 20 miles away, dressing the stones with heavy stone malls.

12
00:01:32.800 --> 00:01:47.680
[George] The builders used sophisticated mortison tenon woodworking joints to lock curved horizontal lintels atop towering upright pillars, perfectly aligning the monument to frame the rising sun on the summer solstice and the setting sun on the winter solstice.

13
00:01:47.680 --> 00:01:50.960
[George] Creating a timeless cathedral to the cosmos.

14
00:01:50.960 --> 00:02:09.960
[George] Modern archaeological excavations at Stonehenge have uncovered hundreds of prehistoric cremations and cattle bones, demonstrating that the site served as an imperial ceremonial center and burial ground where ancient tribes gathered from across Britain for midwinter feasts and solstice astronomical celebrations.

15
00:02:09.960 --> 00:02:29.080
[George] Modern laser scanning and geophysical surveys reveal that the surrounding Salisbury Plain was not an isolated ritual clearing, but the sacred axis of a vast Neolithic ritual landscape crisscrossed by wooden cursus avenues, barrow mounds, and circular ditch enclosures aligned with celestial movements.

16
00:02:29.080 --> 00:02:39.240
[George] By the first millennium before Christ, the British Isles were inhabited by Celtic-speaking tribes, the Brigantes, Catuveloni, Isny, and Siliers.

17
00:02:39.240 --> 00:02:48.520
[George] Living in fortified hillforts encircled by steep earthen ramparts like Maiden Castle in Dorset, the Celts were a fiercely proud, warrior culture.

18
00:02:48.520 --> 00:02:56.360
[George] They were master blacksmiths who forged intricate bronze and iron torques, enameled shields and swift two-wheeled war chariots.

19
00:02:56.360 --> 00:02:59.720
[George] Celtic warfare was psychological and athletic.

20
00:02:59.720 --> 00:03:14.840
[George] Warriors painted their skin with blue woad dye derived from the Asadi's plant, charged into battle, blowing long bronze animal-headed horns called carnyxes that produced terrifying, guttural shrieks, and hurled insults at opposing chieftains.

21
00:03:14.840 --> 00:03:20.520
[George] Guiding Celtic society was a secretive, revered priestly class known as the Druids.

22
00:03:20.520 --> 00:03:29.480
[George] Serving as judges, lore keepers, astronomers, and healers, the Druids committed all sacred knowledge to memory, refusing to write their doctrines.

23
00:03:29.480 --> 00:03:45.760
[George] Gathering in sacred oak groves, especially on the holy island of Mona, modern Anglesey, the Druids conducted rituals beneath the mistletoe, teaching the immortality and transmigration of the soul, maintaining an unshakable spiritual grip over the tribal kings.

24
00:03:46.000 --> 00:03:55.520
[George] Celtic blacksmiths decorated their bronze shields and scabbards with intricate curvilinear latine artwork, embedding glass and coral into the metalwork.

25
00:03:55.520 --> 00:04:05.040
[George] Their sacred reverence for rivers and bogs led them to deposit ceremonial weapons as votive offerings to water spirits, recovered today from the river Thames.

26
00:04:05.040 --> 00:04:25.040
[George] Roman engineers transformed Britannia with astonishing speed, felling old-growth oak forests, digging deep drainage trenches, and constructing heavy timber wharves along the Thames that accommodated trading vessels carrying Gaulish wine, Spanish olive oil, and Italian glassware directly into the infant port of Londinium.

27
00:04:25.040 --> 00:04:40.160
[George] In 55 and 54 BC, Julius Caesar had launched two exploratory military raids into Britain, landing on the pebble beaches of Deal, extracting token tribute from King Casavillanus, and withdrawing across the English Channel.

28
00:04:40.160 --> 00:04:54.480
[George] But nearly a century later, in 43 AD, the stammering Roman Emperor Claudius, desperate for imperial military prestige to secure his precarious throne, ordered the full-scale invasion and annexation of Britannia.

29
00:04:54.480 --> 00:05:07.760
[George] General Aulus Plaudius landed four season Roman legions, the 2nd Augusta, 9th Hispana, 14th Gemina, and 20th Valeria Victrix, totalling 40,000 legionaries and auxiliaries.

30
00:05:07.760 --> 00:05:18.560
[George] At the Battle of the Medway, Roman legionaries swam across the tidal river, crippled the chariot horses of the British kings Caritacus and Togedumnus, and forced the Thames.

31
00:05:18.560 --> 00:05:31.560
[George] Emperor Claudius himself travelled to Britain for sixteen days, bringing sixteen trained Indian war elephants to cross the Thames into Camulogenum, modern Colchester, the premier British tribal stronghold.

32
00:05:31.800 --> 00:05:42.920
[George] Claudius received the formal surrender of eleven British kings, establishing Britannia as a permanent imperial province and founding Roman legionary fortresses across the south.

33
00:05:42.920 --> 00:05:59.240
[George] Emperor Claudius celebrated his British triumph in Rome with unprecedented magnificence, constructing a triumphal arch topped with an equestrian statue and parading captured British prisoners, while renaming his infant son Britannicus in honor of the island conquest.

34
00:05:59.240 --> 00:06:14.920
[George] The humiliation of Queen Boudica was compounded by the Roman confiscation of private estates, the recall of imperial loans made to tribal chieftains by the philosopher Seneca, and the desecration of Isni royal burial sites by greedy Roman tax collectors.

35
00:06:14.920 --> 00:06:27.560
[George] In eastern Britain, across the flat marshlands and fens of modern Norfolk, the Isni tribe had managed to maintain nominal independence as an allied client kingdom under their wealthy king, Prasutagus.

36
00:06:27.560 --> 00:06:38.440
[George] To protect his family and ensure the survival of his realm, Prasutagus drafted a will leaving half his vast royal estate to his two daughters and the other half to the Roman Emperor Nero.

37
00:06:38.440 --> 00:06:44.520
[George] But when Prasutagus died in 60 AD, Roman greed erupted in unrestrained savagery.

38
00:06:44.520 --> 00:06:50.760
[George] Roman centurions and imperial procurator Caddus decianus treated the kingdom as conquered plunder.

39
00:06:50.760 --> 00:07:00.840
[George] The royal estates were confiscated, chief Isni nobles were stripped of their ancestral lands and sold into slavery, and royal relatives were treated as captives.

40
00:07:00.840 --> 00:07:18.720
[George] When Prasutagus's widow, Queen Boudicca, described by Roman historian Cassius Dio as a tall, commanding woman with a fierce gaze, a harsh voice, and thick red hair flowing down to her hips, protested the illegal confiscation, the Romans committed an unforgivable atrocity.

41
00:07:18.960 --> 00:07:25.840
[George] They publicly stripped and flogged Queen Boudicca with leather whips, and brutally violated her two young daughters.

42
00:07:25.840 --> 00:07:30.160
[George] The Roman insult ignited a fury that would bathe the province in blood.

43
00:07:30.160 --> 00:07:46.160
[George] The flogging of Queen Bouddhica was recorded with deep indignation even by Roman historians like Tacitus, who condemned the arrogant brutality of Roman procurators who treated proud royal allies as conquered slaves, sowing the seeds of imperial retribution.

44
00:07:46.160 --> 00:08:02.320
[George] Roman veterans in Camulogenum had treated the native Trinivantes as enslaved beasts, building a colossal limestone temple dedicated to the divine emperor Claudius that functioned as an intolerable financial extortion racket and an insulting symbol of foreign tyranny.

45
00:08:02.320 --> 00:08:07.360
[George] Bouddhica summoned the Isni chieftains to a secret assembly in the Norfolk forests.

46
00:08:07.360 --> 00:08:23.920
[George] Joined by the neighboring Trinivantes, who were seething with rage over the confiscation of their lands for retired Roman veterans and the tyrannical costs of maintaining the massive temple of Claudius at Camulogenum, Boudicca forged an army of over 100,000 tribal warriors.

47
00:08:23.920 --> 00:08:34.480
[George] While Roman governor Suetonius Paulinus was far away on the northwest coast of Wales, eradicating the Druids on the island of Anglesey, Boudicca struck like an avalanche.

48
00:08:34.480 --> 00:08:44.240
[George] The rebel hordes swarmed Camulogenum, butchering the Roman garrison and burning the Temple of Claudius, where citizens held out for two days before being massacred.

49
00:08:44.240 --> 00:08:52.960
[George] When Quintus Petilius Syriales rushed to the rescue with the Ninth Legion, Boudicca ambushed the column, annihilating the Roman infantry.

50
00:08:52.960 --> 00:08:58.800
[George] Marching south, Boudicca descended upon Lindinium, a bustling commercial port on the Thames.

51
00:08:58.800 --> 00:09:06.440
[George] Recognizing he lacked troops to defend the city, Suetonius evacuated, abandoning Lindinium to the flames.

52
00:09:06.440 --> 00:09:18.360
[George] Modern archaeologists excavating London uncover the thick, black Boudican destruction layer, a footdeep stratum of reddened ash and melted Roman glass testifying to the total inferno.

53
00:09:18.360 --> 00:09:34.920
[George] The Boudican destruction layer, visible in archaeological strata across modern London, reaches up to two feet in depth, containing scorched coins, heat-distorted Roman pottery, and charred building beams that confirm the apocalyptic ferocity of the Celtic uprising.

54
00:09:34.920 --> 00:09:38.360
[George] The destruction of Lindinium was total and terrifying.

55
00:09:38.360 --> 00:09:49.880
[George] Modern archaeological excavations beneath the streets of London uncover a distinct, foot thick red layer of charred brick, scorched daub, and ash known as the Boudican Burn Layer.

56
00:09:49.880 --> 00:09:53.320
[George] Physical evidence of the Queen's merciless retribution.

57
00:09:53.320 --> 00:09:57.880
[George] Having levelled Lindinium and the municipium of Verulamium, modern St.

58
00:09:57.880 --> 00:10:08.920
[George] Albans, leaving 70,000 Romans and pro-Roman Britons dead, Boudicca advanced with an overwhelming army numbering over 200,000 warriors, women, and children.

59
00:10:08.920 --> 00:10:13.000
[George] But Governor Suetonius Paulinus was a master tactician.

60
00:10:13.000 --> 00:10:25.960
[George] Gathering 10,000 men from the 14th Legion and veterans of the 20th, Suetonius chose his battlefield with brilliant precision along the Roman road of Wattling Street, likely near Manciter in the West Midlands.

61
00:10:25.960 --> 00:10:37.000
[George] He positioned his army in a narrow defile flanked by dense woodland, with a thick forest at his back, ensuring that the Britons could not outflank him and could only attack from the front.

62
00:10:37.000 --> 00:10:48.000
[George] The British warriors, brimming with overconfidence, arranged hundreds of wooden baggage wagons carrying their wives and families in a giant semicircle behind their lines to watch the slaughter.

63
00:10:48.320 --> 00:10:54.160
[George] Riding past in her chariot, her two daughters beside her, Boudica rallied her warriors.

64
00:10:54.160 --> 00:10:57.040
[George] I am not fighting for my kingdom and wealth now.

65
00:10:57.040 --> 00:11:03.440
[George] I am fighting as an ordinary person for my lost freedom, my bruised body, and my outraged daughters.

66
00:11:03.440 --> 00:11:05.360
[George] Win the battle or perish.

67
00:11:05.360 --> 00:11:07.920
[George] That is what I, a woman, will do.

68
00:11:07.920 --> 00:11:10.960
[George] You men may live on and be slaves if you please.

69
00:11:10.960 --> 00:11:17.040
[George] Roman military superiority on Waddling Street relied on strict echeloned formation discipline.

70
00:11:17.040 --> 00:11:27.760
[George] Legionaries stood shoulder to shoulder, their iron helmets and scudum shields presenting an impenetrable wall against which the unarmoured British warriors shattered in feudal charges.

71
00:11:27.760 --> 00:11:46.480
[George] At the Battle of Wattling Street, the British tribes had brought their wives, children, and baggage wagons to the rear of the field in a vast crescent to witness the slaughter of Rome, but this wagon train inadvertently became an impassable death trap when the panicked warriors were driven backward onto their own families.

72
00:11:46.480 --> 00:11:55.920
[George] As the roaring British horde charged into the narrow defile, Suetonius ordered his legionaries to hold their ground until the enemy was within forty paces.

73
00:11:55.920 --> 00:12:04.320
[George] On command, thousands of heavy Roman pillum javelins whistled through the air, piercing shields and pinning Celtic warriors to the earth.

74
00:12:04.320 --> 00:12:14.800
[George] Drawing their short gladius swords, the Roman legionaries locked their curved shields into an impenetrable wedge formation, advancing with rhythmic, robotic discipline.

75
00:12:14.800 --> 00:12:18.800
[George] In the narrow killing ground, the British numbers worked against them.

76
00:12:18.800 --> 00:12:23.040
[George] Crowded together, warriors could not swing their long broadswords.

77
00:12:23.040 --> 00:12:26.640
[George] The Roman wedge drove into the mob like an iron plough.

78
00:12:26.640 --> 00:12:33.480
[George] When the Britons broke and turned to flee, their escape was completely blocked by their own circle of family wagons.

79
00:12:33.480 --> 00:12:42.440
[George] Roman legionaries showed no mercy, slaughtering 80,000 Britons, including the women and pack animals, while losing only 400 Romans.

80
00:12:42.440 --> 00:12:52.440
[George] Bouddhica escaped the carnage, but rather than suffer the humiliation of being paraded in chains through the Forum of Rome, the rebel queen ended her life by poison.

81
00:12:52.440 --> 00:13:07.640
[George] Though Roman rule was secured for three more centuries, building Hadrian's wall across the northern frontier, Boudicca's fiery defiance endured as the immortal symbol of British freedom, celebrated in bronze on the Thames beside the Houses of Parliament.

82
00:13:07.640 --> 00:13:24.040
[George] Boudicca's heroic legacy was resurrected during the Victorian era, when Queen Victoria saw in her namesake an ancient symbol of female British sovereignty, culminating in the magnificent bronze statue of Boudicca and her daughters riding their war chariot beside Westminster Bridge.

83
00:13:24.040 --> 00:13:36.120
[George] Through the centuries of Saxon invasion, Viking raids, and the rise of the British Empire, the spirit of Boudicca was transformed from a savage Celtic barbarian into a majestic national heroine.

84
00:13:36.120 --> 00:13:44.040
[George] Immortalized in Thomas Thornecroft's colossal bronze statue beside Westminster Bridge, opposite the Houses of Parliament.

85
00:13:44.520 --> 00:13:53.240
[Rebecca] From the megaliths of Stonehenge to the ashes of Lindinium, ancient Britain forged its spirit in resistance against the mighty Roman Empire.

86
00:13:53.240 --> 00:13:58.200
[Rebecca] A thousand years later, a different invasion would remake the island forever.

87
00:13:58.200 --> 00:14:02.920
[Rebecca] Next week we continue our United Kingdom saga with episode 213.

88
00:14:02.920 --> 00:14:08.280
[Rebecca] 1066, The Norman Conquest, the Battle of Hastings, and the Doomsday Book.

89
00:14:08.280 --> 00:14:18.320
[Rebecca] The three-way claim to the English Crown, the March of King Harold, the Bloody shield wall on Senlac Hill, the arrow that struck an eye, and the survey that catalogued an entire kingdom.

90
00:14:18.560 --> 00:14:23.200
[Rebecca] Until then, thank you for listening to most fascinating stories.

